To terminate a C program, you can use the `exit()` function from the standard library `<stdlib.h>`. This function terminates the execution of the program and returns an integer value to the environment from which the program was executed.

Here's a simple example:

```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>

int main() {
    printf("Hello, World!\n");

    // Terminate the program
    exit(0);  // The argument 0 typically indicates successful termination

    // Note: Code after exit() will not be executed
    return 0; // This line is unreachable
}
```

